WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.400
When you think of the most dominant&nbsp;
creatures on earth - what comes to mind?
 &nbsp;

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.560
If considering sheer size, perhaps you&nbsp;
immediately thought of the ocean - and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:16.880
largest animal that’s ever lived - the blue whale.&nbsp;
Or perhaps thinking of strength and ferocity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:23.760
you imagined lush forests full of fearsome big&nbsp;
cats. Or maybe, quite sensibly, you thought of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:29.920
us - human beings- the most intelligent, and one&nbsp;
of the most widespread creatures on the planet. 
 &nbsp;

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:34.800
But if you were to walk down the sidewalk, or lean&nbsp;
against a tree, the first animal that you would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:42.000
likely encounter is something small - ants. Their&nbsp;
total population is around ten thousand trillion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:47.280
and when combined their total weight would be&nbsp;
about the same as the weight of all human beings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.960
in biomass and in impact on ecosystems,&nbsp;
these small animals are arguably the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:58.640
successful creatures to have ever lived.  
 
Ants have colonized almost every landmass on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:04.240
earth. Their presence is so significant that they&nbsp;
have directed the evolution of countless other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:11.920
plant and animal species. Aggressive, warlike,&nbsp;
but also cooperative, altruistic - ants living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:18.080
in colonies exhibit some of the most complex&nbsp;
behaviors of all insects. And the colony is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:24.320
only thing that matters in the lives of ants.&nbsp;
Their loyalty to it is absolute. And in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:30.000
service to it, they exhibit some of the most&nbsp;
complex social behavior in the animal kingdom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.160
giving rise to their nearly complete&nbsp;
ecological conquest of the earth.  
 &nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:41.280
How do ant societies work, and what is it about&nbsp;
the colony that has made ants so successful?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:46.000
Is it as simple as “strength in numbers?”&nbsp;
Or is it something more profound?
 &nbsp;

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:55.520
Even though insects were among the first&nbsp;
animals to colonize land, cooperative insect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:01.520
societies are a relatively recent development. 
 
The first insects emerged around 400 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:06.480
years ago - but ants didn’t emerge until&nbsp;
much later, around 100 million years ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:12.320
They lived amongst the dinosaurs, and for a long&nbsp;
time did not rule the landscape. This was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:18.080
era of the giant dragonflies, cockroaches, and&nbsp;
termites. It wasn’t until 60 million years ago&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:24.160
that they became the dominant insects. And since&nbsp;
then, they have truly flourished. Today there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:30.400
around 16,000 different species of ant, and they&nbsp;
are found on every continent except Antarctica. 
 &nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:35.360
The competitive edge that allowed ants&nbsp;
to become a world-dominant group is their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:39.600
highly developed colonial existence&nbsp;
and specialized social structure. 
 &nbsp;

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:46.320
Ant societies are broken up into two castes: a&nbsp;
non-reproductive worker caste, and a reproductive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:52.640
royal caste. The queen is the only reproducing&nbsp;
female of the entire colony, and the colony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:58.480
exists to serve her, and her alone. The birth&nbsp;
of a colony begins with the birth of a queen. 
 &nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:04.560
The life of a queen begins when its mother queen&nbsp;
lays a special kind of egg, different from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.920
millions of regular worker eggs the queen ant&nbsp;
will lay in her lifetime. These special eggs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:15.200
are laid when the colony passes a certain size -&nbsp;
and when they hatch, they produce ants that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:21.360
larger than regular workers and have wings. These&nbsp;
are the reproductive males and young queens. 
 &nbsp;

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:26.320
During what is called a nuptial flight&nbsp;
these winged ants take to the air and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:30.960
mate with each other, after which the males&nbsp;
die and the females drop to the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:36.480
scrape off their wings and begin to search for&nbsp;
a place to dig their nest. Few get far in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:41.360
journey. Predators snag most of these young&nbsp;
queens before they can establish themselves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:48.160
Only around 1 in 500 new queens has a chance&nbsp;
at success. But those that do succeed become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:54.160
the single egg-laying queen of their new colony.
 
Over the course of her life, a queen ant can lay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.160 --> 00:04:00.320
up to 300 million eggs, depending on the species&nbsp;
of ant. And the vast majority of these eggs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:06.000
hatch into dedicated worker ants - female ants&nbsp;
whose lives are devoted to the queen’s welfare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:11.520
and reproductive activity. At any given time,&nbsp;
there are millions of worker ants maintaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:17.120
the colony. There can be many different types,&nbsp;
and many different sizes of workers even within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:23.360
a single colony. Some care for larvae, others&nbsp;
excavate tunnels, others build amazing structures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:28.800
while others leave the nest and search for&nbsp;
food, or go to war with neighboring colonies. 
 &nbsp;

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:34.560
And surprisingly, it is the ant workers themselves&nbsp;
who ultimately decide the fate of the young,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:38.400
choosing which worker caste they&nbsp;
will develop into. Larvae develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:41.840
into different types of workers based&nbsp;
largely on the nutrition they receive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:47.440
Those fed more insects than seeds are&nbsp;
more likely to become larger individuals.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:53.440
For leaf cutter ants, the polymorphism&nbsp;
that exists among the workers is extreme.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:58.560
The largest workers, called soldiers, defend&nbsp;
the colony, while the medium sized workers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:03.600
collect leaves, excavate tunnels, or collect&nbsp;
garbage, and the smallest workers raise the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:08.160
young and cultivate and farm fungus - the&nbsp;
only food source for the whole colony. 
 &nbsp;

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:14.560
The workers toil away in a flawless synchrony&nbsp;
- in a flow that looks chaotic to our eyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:18.160
but is in fact based on a&nbsp;
complex system of communication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:23.840
a chemical and physical language invisible&nbsp;
to us, but binds these societies together. 
 &nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:30.720
For years, scientists knew that ants must&nbsp;
have some way to talk to each other in order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:35.600
to organize their intricate societies.&nbsp;
But ants have poor vision and hearing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:41.440
so scientists knew that ant communication must&nbsp;
work in a fundamentally different way to ours. 
 &nbsp;

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:48.320
It was a mystery for years, but in 1962 leading&nbsp;
entomologist EO Wilson began to crack the code&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:53.040
of ant communication. He started with the&nbsp;
question of how ants tell other members of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:59.280
the colony the location of a new food source. He&nbsp;
noticed that ants often tap their abdomen to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:04.080
ground when travelling, so wondered if they&nbsp;
were leaving some sort of chemical trail. 
 &nbsp;

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:09.520
He began painstakingly dissecting fire ant&nbsp;
abdomens, crushing each of the organs with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:14.080
an applicator stick. He would then spread&nbsp;
the ant juice on a piece of paper in front&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:19.360
of other fire ants, to see if they would react.&nbsp;
As he worked through each of the known organs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:24.320
the ants showed no interest. But then he stumbled&nbsp;
upon an organ that had never been studied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:30.240
before - something called Dufour’s gland. When he&nbsp;
crushed this gland and spread it across the paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:35.280
the ants went wild. They followed the&nbsp;
trail immediately and completely.
 &nbsp;

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:42.000
Over time, scientists have discovered over 20&nbsp;
different pheromones that ants use to communicate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:48.000
And by combining different signals, ants&nbsp;
have created a complex pheromonal language.
 &nbsp;

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:53.840
African weaver ants, in particular, have the most&nbsp;
sophisticated pheromone communication system ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:59.360
studied in animals. Some of their thoughts are&nbsp;
expressed by spreading pheromones on the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:02.320
like previously mentioned,&nbsp;
combined with physical gestures. 
 &nbsp;

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:08.080
When a worker wishes to say ‘follow me I have&nbsp;
found some food’ she deposits a trail from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:13.520
rectal gland, while running from the food back&nbsp;
to the nest. When she encounters other workers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:18.560
she waves her head and touches the other ant&nbsp;
with her two antennae. Or when a worker wishes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:23.760
to raise the alarm about an enemy, she lays&nbsp;
short looping trails around the intruder with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:27.600
secretions from the sternal gland. 
 
Other signals are sent through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:32.800
air. When an African weaver ant worker&nbsp;
encounters an enemy in her own territory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:38.400
she releases a mixture of four chemicals that&nbsp;
not only convey a message but elicit a response&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:44.080
from all other workers in her vicinity. 
 
The first tells the other ants to ‘be alert’.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:48.160
The next one tells them to search for the&nbsp;
trouble. The next one tells them to come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:53.760
closer and bite anything in their path, and the&nbsp;
final compound tells them to go nuts and attack.
 &nbsp;

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:59.600
Scientists believe that the combination of&nbsp;
these signals very closely resembles syntax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:05.200
that we see in human language. A main reason ants&nbsp;
are so successful in the world then, is the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:11.840
reason that humans are. Like us, communication&nbsp;
gives ants the amazing capacity to cooperate. 
 &nbsp;

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:19.040
Of all ants, weaver ants are among the most&nbsp;
impressive. They dominate the forests in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:24.880
Africa and Australia, in large part due to their&nbsp;
complex and efficient chemical communication.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:28.960
But perhaps more remarkable than their&nbsp;
ability to communicate so effectively,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:33.280
is what they can achieve with it. Weaver&nbsp;
ants are a species of ant that do not live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:38.640
in or on the ground, but in the trees. And&nbsp;
to keep their huge populations safe there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:43.680
they construct their own housing. They weave&nbsp;
branches and leaves together to create an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:49.920
architectural feat, full of a network of different&nbsp;
rooms complete with roofs, walls, and floors. 
 &nbsp;

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:55.600
To begin the process, a single ant searches for&nbsp;
a nice, bendy looking leaf. It will pull on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.600 --> 00:09:00.880
edges, testing to see if the leaf will curl.&nbsp;
If the ant has some measure of success, other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:06.880
ants will be attracted to the endeavor, and begin&nbsp;
pulling the edge as well. As the leaf bends more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:13.120
more workers arrive. They line up in precise rows,&nbsp;
gripping the edge, pulling it towards another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:19.360
leaf. If the gap is too large for a single row of&nbsp;
ants to seal, they perform an impressive acrobatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:26.240
tactic: they chain their bodies together to form&nbsp;
a bridge. Workers climb down the bodies of others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:32.160
until the chain can reach the other leaf edge, up&nbsp;
to 10 workers long. Once the leaf edges are within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:37.840
reach, the workers move into position to seal the&nbsp;
leaves together. But what looks like glue on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:43.920
finished structure is something more surprising.
 
Once the bent leaves are ready to be sealed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:48.080
the workers will collect larvae who are&nbsp;
in their final stages of development,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:53.840
and use their threads of silk to bind the leaves&nbsp;
together. Holding the larvae in their mandibles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:59.280
the workers move the larvae back and forth across&nbsp;
the leaf edges - using their babies like a hot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:04.640
glue gun. The larvae seem okay with this, as they&nbsp;
respond to this motion by exuding thousands of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:10.480
threads of silk. This silk becomes a sheet between&nbsp;
the edges, and works as a powerful adhesive.
 &nbsp;

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:16.240
Structures like this speak to ants capacity&nbsp;
to organize their labor effectively, millions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:21.680
of individuals combining their abilities into&nbsp;
something much greater. It’s easy to assume, then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:26.400
that their effectiveness comes down to a strength&nbsp;
in numbers, along with the communication to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:32.320
orchestrate the work. But this can’t account for&nbsp;
all of their behavior, and all of their success.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:37.600
There is something more unifying in the world of&nbsp;
the ants. And the individual ant’s cooperation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:45.840
with one another is so profound, that it makes&nbsp;
scientists rethink the idea of individuality.
 &nbsp;

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:54.800
The power of a group is evident - with more ants&nbsp;
working together, they can find food more quickly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:59.360
build more impressive structures, and&nbsp;
defend against enemies more effectively.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:04.880
And working together is common in nature -&nbsp;
birds in flocks, or bison in herds live longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:09.920
when they live in groups. But in cooperative&nbsp;
animal groups like these, individuals still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:14.800
look out for their personal interests.&nbsp;
For them it's not just about the group.
 &nbsp;

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:20.640
But this is not the case for ants. Worker ants&nbsp;
die young, and usually don’t create offspring.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:26.880
Their existence is sacrificial. They have no self&nbsp;
interest. Certain ants have been found to suffer a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:33.120
death rate of 6% per hour when outside the nest,&nbsp;
due to fighting with neighboring colonies. On&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:39.760
average each forager survives for only a week. But&nbsp;
during that time, she manages to collect 20 times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:45.120
her own body weight in food for the colony - all&nbsp;
to support the group, and ultimately, the queen. 
 &nbsp;

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:50.800
This unwavering loyalty to the queen,&nbsp;
and the self sacrifice to her cause,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:56.320
becomes more evident when a queen dies. Logic&nbsp;
would assume that when the queen dies the workers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.320 --> 00:12:02.560
would raise another queen to replace her. But this&nbsp;
is not at all what the workers do. In most cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:08.800
the colony fails to produce a royal successor,&nbsp;
and it declines until the last worker dies. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:14.800
simply do nothing until there is no one left. 
 
This level of altruism and self sacrifice is so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:18.880
rare in the animal kingdom, that it has&nbsp;
made scientists rethink what it means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:24.720
to be an individual. If ant individuals in a&nbsp;
colony are not competing against each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:30.720
if they are bound tightly by communication and a&nbsp;
caste-division of labor, if they cannot survive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:35.520
out of the colony for very long - does the&nbsp;
concept of the individual break down? 
 &nbsp;

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:41.760
The idea of the superorganism is one that has&nbsp;
been debated for decades. With ants, it's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:47.200
idea that the colony is the organism, where the&nbsp;
queen is the reproductive organ, the workers the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:53.440
supporting brain, heart, and gut. The exchange of&nbsp;
food among the workers is like the circulation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:59.520
blood. [6] The most advanced ant societies, like&nbsp;
weaver ants, driver ants, or leafcutter ants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:03.840
fall into this category, where their workers&nbsp;
do not compete amongst themselves at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:09.920
and do not reproduce outside of royalty.
 
This capacity of the colony to act like a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:16.000
superorganism has made scientists reconsider&nbsp;
evolutionary theory as a whole. In the 1960s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:20.880
and 70s, the conventional way of thinking&nbsp;
about evolution was centered around genes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:27.600
and genes alone. Largely popularized by Dawkin’s&nbsp;
book The Selfish gene, it follows that the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:32.960
two individuals are genetically related, the more&nbsp;
sense it makes for them to behave selflessly with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:38.400
each other - that all altruistic group behavior&nbsp;
comes down to each individual’s competitive desire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:44.640
to improve chances of their kin’s survival. 
 
But some ant biologists, like EO Wilson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:50.400
believed that this couldn’t be the whole story.&nbsp;
Instincts from social species like ants go far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:56.320
beyond the urge to protect their immediate kin.&nbsp;
The group must also have a role in evolution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.320 --> 00:14:01.280
whether or not the group members are related&nbsp;
to each other. And this idea gave rise to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:06.400
theory of multi-level evolution, or group&nbsp;
selection, where natural selection acts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:10.480
at the level of the group, instead of at the&nbsp;
more conventional level of the individual. 
 &nbsp;

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:17.360
The way ant societies function, in their daily&nbsp;
lives and within evolution, has entranced us as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:22.880
humans for decades. They have created heated&nbsp;
debates among the world’s top scientists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:28.480
and been the focus of every kid’s backyard&nbsp;
curiosity. Their world operates in ways our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:33.840
brains can barely conceive - with chemical&nbsp;
signals painted on the ground, instincts that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:40.640
drive them to fatal endeavors - and yet, their&nbsp;
sociality, cooperation, and complexity in many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:47.120
ways mirrors our own. Studying ants will continue&nbsp;
to reveal answers about the nature of evolution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.120 --> 00:14:53.040
and in turn, will reveal answers about our&nbsp;
own society, and our own individuality. 
 &nbsp;

00:14:54.160 --> 00:14:59.920
Studying the animal kingdom gives insight into&nbsp;
our world, and brings researchers on all sorts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.920 --> 00:15:05.920
of journeys - both intellectual and physical. Some&nbsp;
of the research about ants happens in the field,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.920 --> 00:15:10.080
in the steamy tropics or the&nbsp;
frigid parts of northern Finland.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:14.480
And some of it is done in the lab, with&nbsp;
complex apparatuses engineered to study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:19.280
ant’s chemical trails or their inclination to&nbsp;
go to war with each other. Hearing about what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.280 --> 00:15:25.920
it takes to carry out research like this is often&nbsp;
just as interesting as the science itself. I love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.920 --> 00:15:31.280
hearing the human stories behind the science -&nbsp;
and this is why we decided to start a podcast. 
 &nbsp;

00:15:31.840 --> 00:15:36.800
Modulus - hosted by me, and Brian from&nbsp;
Real Engineering, is a podcast about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:42.080
people behind the science we explore here on&nbsp;
YouTube. We talk to the scientists who are on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.080 --> 00:15:46.880
the cutting edge of research, and the people&nbsp;
who are affected by the topics we discuss. 
 &nbsp;

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:54.320
The second episode of Modulus is out now. It’s an&nbsp;
episode where I talk to two pioneers of the ocean:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.320 --> 00:16:00.000
some of the world’s first saturation divers. They&nbsp;
discuss what it’s like to live at the bottom of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:03.997
the ocean, and how it affects both your body and&nbsp;
your mind. Their personal accounts of the effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.997 --> 00:16:04.399
of the immense pressure on their bodies very much&nbsp;
makes you realize that the profession of deep sea&nbsp;&nbsp;

